1. **Outside view.** The historical data shows a relatively stable share for these five brands, occasionally interrupted by product announcements or major events. Before the September iPhone launch, the baseline shares were roughly: iPhone ~40%, Disney ~20%, Netflix ~17%, Samsung ~12%, and Tesla ~9%. The September iPhone launch spiked its share to ~54%, squashing the relative shares of the other four brands, before gradually settling back to ~41% by early October. A similar dynamic occurred with Tesla in mid-October, spiking its share to over 18% before falling to 13.28% a week later. A naive extrapolation would predict these spikes continue returning to their respective baseline levels, expecting iPhone around 41%, Tesla ~9%, and the rest naturally reverting to their pre-spike means.

2. **Inside view.** Two major factors stand out for the Nov 1 - 7 target week. First, the recent Tesla spike (related to Roadster news mid-October) should fully decay by early November, removing its dampening effect on the rest of the basket and returning Tesla to ~9.0%. Second, Apple has the "iPhone Duo" (a potential new premium foldable) set to launch in stores. With news of its retail release arriving around Oct 22, it will likely see strong search interest in late October through early November. This means the iPhone share is unlikely to just drift down to 40%, but will likely remain elevated at around 43.5%. The remaining 47.5% of the basket will be distributed among Samsung (historically robust but lacking immediate launch catalysts), Netflix, and Disney. Normalizing their baselines around the expected iPhone and Tesla levels puts Netflix at ~17.9%, Disney at ~18.7%, and Samsung at ~10.9%.

3. **Pre-mortem.** The biggest risk is the exact launch date of the iPhone Duo. If the phone actually drops during the Nov 1 - Nov 7 window, iPhone searches could surge back toward 50% (as seen during the Sept rollout). This would artificially suppress the shares of all other brands by another 10-20% below our baseline expectations. To correct for this, I will raise the expected standard deviation significantly for the iPhone (to capture the upside risk of a massive spike) and for the other brands (to account for the corresponding downside squeeze).

4. **Calibrate.** Standard deviations need to absorb the typical weekly noise as well as the potential for event-driven volatility. An SD of 3.5 for iPhone appropriately covers outcomes from 40% (a quiet week) up to 47% (strong launch week). The other brands get standard deviations scaled to their typical percentage variance, around 1.0 to 1.5, allowing enough room for the mechanical shifts depending on how the iPhone performs.

